                <!-- EXPRESSION VS STATEMENT -->

                <h3 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-5 mb-3">
                    Expression Lambda vs Statement Lambda
                </h3>

                <div class="table-responsive mb-4">
                    <table class="table table-bordered">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Type</th>
                                <th>Example</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Expression Lambda</td>
                                <td>number =&gt; number * 2</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Statement Lambda</td>
                                <td>number =&gt; { return number * 2; }</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>


                <!-- ADVANTAGES -->

                <h3 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-5 mb-3">
                    Advantages of Lambda Expressions
                </h3>

                <ul class="text-muted">
                    <li>Reduces unnecessary code.</li>
                    <li>Makes short functions easier to read.</li>
                    <li>Works naturally with delegates.</li>
                    <li>Widely used with LINQ.</li>
                    <li>Useful for collection filtering and sorting.</li>
                    <li>Supports functional-style programming.</li>
                </ul>


                <!-- COMMON MISTAKES -->

                <h3 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-5 mb-3">
                    Common Mistakes
                </h3>

                <ul class="text-muted">
                    <li>Using a lambda where a normal named method would be clearer.</li>
                    <li>Using overly complex lambda expressions.</li>
                    <li>Forgetting the correct parameter types when type inference is not enough.</li>
                    <li>Confusing Action and Func.</li>
                    <li>Writing difficult-to-read nested lambda expressions.</li>
                </ul>


